+# Diary — 2026-10-25 (4)
+
+## A running container holds an activity, keeps its output in a file, and a token can read it
+
+The first run of a container machine from the built app (a debug build, a scratch data folder, driven only through the control
+service) worked but showed a defect and left questions. Five commits follow from it. Each was gated (8/8) on its own tree before
+it was made; the first three were rebuilt after a review and gated again. No virtual machine was started for them and the real
+runner was never run: everything marked TESTED below comes from tests with fake runner scripts, scratch folders and local
+sockets. The rows marked SEEN come from the real run of the same day, which is not reproducible from the repository.
+
+| # | Claim | Probe | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | A container machine starts from the built app, reaches `running` and ends `stopped` with no runner left | the real run: two machines, driven by the control client | SEEN |
+| 2 | Costs: cold about 63 s (a kernel archive of about 290 MB for each machine, and the image), warm 3 to 9 s | the same run | SEEN |
+| 3 | A forced stop answers in 0.44 s and the runner is gone at 0.60 s; ending the app with SIGTERM leaves no runner | the same run | SEEN |
+| 4 | A stop that asks takes 72 s to reach `stopped`, not 15 s at most | the same run | SEEN (the defect) |
+| 5 | That is App Nap coalescing the timers of the stop escalation | no run with the change; only a candidate | NOT RUN |
+| 6 | A running container holds one user-initiated activity, not latency-critical, from the start of its runner to its end, and one that is released early ends it | a spy in place of the process: start, stop, a start that throws, a runner that fails before it is ready, a workload released while it runs | TESTED |
+| 7 | The stop path logs each step with the runner's process identifier | the lines are written by the code the tests run; no test reads the log | NOT RUN |
+| 8 | A run's output is kept in a private file of the machine's folder, bounded to 1 MiB with one previous file, filtered as the pane is | scratch folders: creation with mode 0600, two runs appending, rotation, an append bigger than the limit, a cut at a character | TESTED |
+| 9 | A link, a file open to others, a file with a second name or a pipe at the path is left alone and never holds the writer; reading them gives nothing | the same tests with each of them planted | TESTED |
+| 10 | The note of dropped bytes survives the default limits, and two runs writing at once lose nothing across a rotation | a held queue; two file objects on the same paths | TESTED |
+| 11 | Duplicate does not copy the output, deleting the machine removes it | the library with a container machine | TESTED |
+| 12 | `machines.log` needs the log capability and the usual checks, answers at most 64 KiB, and only for a container with agent access on | the dispatcher, the app's adapter with a container and a virtual machine, an organisation that keeps the service off | TESTED |
+| 13 | A 64 KiB tail of the characters that grow most under JSON escaping arrives exactly and fits what the client accepts | a connection and a client over a pair of sockets | TESTED |
+| 14 | `vmr control log` and the MCP tool `machines_log` return the log | the built `vmr` against a service on a socket; an MCP session over pipes | TESTED |
+| 15 | The control listener closes every connection it accepts and leaves no descriptor open | seven ways a connection ends, then 128 mixed connections and a count of the descriptors | TESTED |
+| 16 | The app's own descriptors do not grow | 44 at rest, 54 after a container ran and was stopped, unexplained | NOT SEEN |
+| 17 | The container's output appears in the app's window | the window cannot be reached by automation in an app that was never activated | NOT SEEN |
+| 18 | The quit through the menu with a container running stops and kills the runner | the app stayed up behind a confirmation that could not be read | NOT SEEN |
+| 19 | An older app refuses a token file that lists `log` | read from the code (the capability is unknown, so the list cannot be read); not run | NOT RUN |
+
+### What was built
+
+- The workload holds an activity while its runner lives, and the process logs the steps of a stop (category `container`).
+- Each container machine keeps its output in `container-output.log` and `container-output.1.log` in its folder. A drain on one
+  queue shared by all files writes it; the app's main actor never waits for the disk.
+- The control service has a read-only method `machines.log` and a capability `log`; the app reads the end of the files off the
+  main actor; `vmr control log MACHINE [--bytes N] [--json]` and the fifth MCP tool print or return it. A container's exposure
+  summary says so when agent access is on.
+- A test that every path of the control listener closes the connection it accepted.
+
+### Not verified
+
+- Whether the activity shortens the stop. Nothing was run with it, and App Nap is a candidate, not a finding.
+- Anything on a real guest after these changes: the output file with a real run, the stop with its logs.
+- The log lines themselves: no test reads them.
+- Why the app's descriptors grew from 44 to 54; the listener is not the explanation the tests can rule in.
+- The output in the window and the menu quit, which automation could not reach.
+- The placeholder bundle identifier is shared with an installed copy of the app (the defaults and data-directory override
+  domains); the run wrote only the override domain. That is an owner decision, not a code defect.
+
+### Next
+
+Run the stop again with a build that has the activity and the logs, and read the log; look at the quit alert with the app
+active, by the owner; take a breakdown of the app's descriptors before and after a container run.
